<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Privacy manifest (SHIP-04).

  This is the machine-readable half of the App Privacy declaration. The prose half lives in
  docs/store/app-store-listing.md and App Store Connect; this file is what Apple's tooling reads,
  and what turns "we collect nothing" from a marketing claim into something checkable.

  Note on urgency, since it was originally filed as a submission blocker and is not one: build 2
  was uploaded on 2026-07-23 with 44 UserDefaults call sites and no manifest, and App Store
  Connect accepted it. So the absence does not stop an upload. It is still correct to declare,
  both because the required-reason APIs genuinely are in use and because a missing manifest makes
  the nutrition label unverifiable.

  NSPrivacyTracking is false and NSPrivacyTrackingDomains is empty because there is no tracking
  anywhere in the app: no analytics SDK, no crash reporter, no third-party SDK of any kind
  (`grep` for the usual vendors returns nothing). NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is empty because the
  vendor receives nothing — journeys and photos live in the customer's own iCloud, which the
  developer cannot read.

  One honest caveat that belongs with the App Store Connect answers rather than here: publishing a
  journey to the public showcase writes thumbnails and metadata into the developer's own CloudKit
  PUBLIC database, which the developer can read. That is owner-initiated, explicitly consented, and
  world-readable by design — but it means the Connect questionnaire's User Content section must not
  be answered "Data Not Collected" for customers who publish. This manifest describes what the app
  collects on its own initiative, which is nothing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
  <false/>

  <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
  <array/>

  <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
  <array/>

  <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
  <array>
    <!--
      UserDefaults — 44 call sites across 11 files. All of it is this app's own state: the sync
      engine's serialized CKSyncEngine state, the onboarding and demo-seed flags, the appearance
      preference, the developer-tools unlock, comment author name. Never another app's data and
      never read from a shared container for identification purposes, which is exactly what
      CA92.1 declares.
    -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>CA92.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>

    <!--
      File timestamps — FileManager.attributesOfItem at Import/CloudKitImportSink.swift:656.
      Reads only the file's own size/attributes for import accounting, on files inside the app's
      own container. C617.1 is the "inside the app container" reason.
    -->
    <dict>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
      <string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
      <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
      <array>
        <string>C617.1</string>
      </array>
    </dict>
  </array>
</dict>
</plist>
